<!-- GridWindow: the merged "Continue" screen (lib/ui/gridwindow.py).

     ONE ROW PER BAND, ALL ON ONE SCREEN. Each band (resume / next up /
     watched / library) has its own labelled header and its own horizontal
     poster row, and the four rows are stacked in a vertical `grouplist`
     (30001) that scrolls as a unit - so two bands are visible at once and
     the rest are one nudge away. This is Kodi's own idiom for sectioned
     content: skin.estuary's Home builds its widget rows exactly this way
     (`WidgetListPoster` inside `WidgetGroupListCommon`, each preceded by
     a `CategoryLabel`).

     It replaces two rejected attempts, recorded here so they are not
     retried:
       - Header rows interleaved into ONE wrapping `panel`. A panel gives
         every row the item layout's full height, so each header cost a
         whole poster row of screen.
       - One band at a time behind a tab strip. It hid three quarters of
         the screen to label four groups, and read worse than the flat
         grid it replaced.

     `usecontrolcoords` is what makes the stack work: each control is laid
     out directly under the previous one, so a row whose list is empty
     collapses entirely - both its header and its panel are `visible` on
     that list's own NumItems. A viewer with nothing finished sees no
     "Recently watched" heading at all, rather than an empty band.

     Every row shares one cell design: a 208x312 poster, its progress
     track, then the title and badge lines, 396 tall in a 268-wide cell.
     A row is 42 (header) + 396 + 10 (gap) = 448, so TWO whole rows fit
     between the stack's top at 146 and the bottom margin - a third row
     peeks in, which is what shows there is more below. THE CELL LAYOUTS
     ARE GENERATED: all four rows carry a byte-identical copy, because
     this skin has no Includes.xml to share one through. Keep them in
     sync - tests/test_gridwindow.py asserts they are identical.

     The poster box is a true 2:3, so `aspectratio=keep` draws a standard
     poster edge to edge inside it. That is what lets the 208px progress
     track and the labels line up with the ARTWORK rather than with a
     wider box - at an earlier 240 box the rendered poster was only 216
     wide and the bar overhung it by 12px a side.
     `gridwindow.PROGRESS_TRACK_WIDTH` must equal that 208.

     Band identity is carried by colour as well as by the headers - the
     badge is tinted per band (gridwindow._BAND_COLOURS), the header takes
     the same tint, and finished titles are drawn dimmed - so a card still
     says which band it belongs to once its header has scrolled off. -->
